WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:06.810
Film Courage: How much of you being a screenwriter
is about the love of writing so that a movie

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:08.280
can be made from that?

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:14.420
Is it something that is painstaking for you
where 10 hours will go by and you won’t

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:15.720
think of a line of dialogue?

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:18.520
Or is it something where you love the process?

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:21.650
Or you love it because it produces a movie?

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:26.140
Matthew Berkowitz, Filmmaker: Interesting…both.

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:27.699
I love the writing process.

00:00:27.699 --> 00:00:36.040
I really do and I love being able to paint
pictures with words.

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:37.400
I love writing a story.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:41.820
I love being able to tell a story over three
acts or two acts.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:42.900
You can write a two-act film.

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:44.340
You can write these great characters.

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:46.230
I love writing.

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:49.400
Writing is such a passion for me.

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:58.180
But it’s also if you want to say something
there is not a better person to say it than

00:00:58.180 --> 00:00:59.180
yourself.

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:06.000
So as a director sometimes you are forced
to write your own words because you want to

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:07.980
get your vision on screen.

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:11.500
So it’s never really come down to me trying
to create a product.

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:15.800
That’s not what screenwriting is to me to
create a product to go make a movie.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:21.000
So that’s not really my intention as a screenwriter
but it’s always about me telling a story

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.880
I want to tell.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:27.270
And I’ve co-written stuff with buddies and
friends and other writers.

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:32.620
But I really like at times being able to say
what you want to say as a director, what you

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:35.280
want to put on screen.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:38.060
It doesn’t mean I don’t want to direct
other people’s words because I think that

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:40.710
is just as valuable.

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:48.622
As a necessity as a writer/director you have
a vision that you want to write that is in

00:01:48.622 --> 00:01:53.050
your mind some place, that’s in the back
of your head that you can tap these beautiful

00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:59.780
places and tell these beautiful characters
or scary characters and it’s easy to translate

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:02.810
it from your brain to your fingers to the
screen.

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:05.010
Film Courage: That’s interesting.

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:09.920
So it’s about getting out what you want
to say not about not having this thing where

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:12.530
“Look this is a movie and it’s screening
at this festival.”

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:15.880
It’s a burning desire to make a statement.

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:17.209
Matthew: Yes.

00:02:17.209 --> 00:02:19.580
Film Courage: Have you always been like this?

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:24.910
Matthew: I’m sure if you asked my parents
they would probably say yes.

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:29.210
I’ve always been the one who wants to scream
really loud so…

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:35.250
Film Courage: Well you know certain kids are
more precocious and they have a statement

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:37.110
they want to proclaim to the world.

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:39.410
And then others were sort of the quiet ones.

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:40.410
So I just wasn’t sure.

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:41.670
Matthew: No I was the first one.

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:43.510
Yes I was hands down the first one.

